      "text": "We are given the polynomial $f(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0$ and need to find all integer values of $a$ such that the polynomial has at least one integer root. Let's denote the integer root by $b$. Thus, one of the roots of the polynomial equation will be $b$, and we can use polynomial division to find the remainder when we express the polynomial in terms of a integer root.\n\nComplete the polynomial root equation using synthetic division to utilize the given datatype of answers:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(b - k) \\cdot (b^2 + 3ab + 3a^2)}{b(x - k)}\n\\]\nWhen $b$ is evaluated at 0, the function:\n\n\\[\nf(k) = 7k^3 + 3ak^2 + ak\n\\]\n\nmust also be equal to zero (since $k$ is canceled out in the polynomial root equation). So we have:\n\n\\[\n7x^3 + 3ax^2 + ax + 7 = 0\n\\]\n\nLet's analyze the factor combination based upon the polynomial expression. We want to streamline the polynomial by checking the factor possibilities of the integer values. \n\n**At 1 and -1:**\n\n$f(1) = 1 + 3 + a + 7 = 11 + a = 0 $\n$f(-1) = -1 + 3 - a + 7 = 9 - a = 0 $ \n\nSolving these equations:\n\n\\[\na = -11 \\text{ and } a = 9\n\\]\n\nTherefore: \n\n\\boxed{-11, 9}",
